{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-351724" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with velocity $v(t)={csc}^{2}⁡(t),$ where $t>0$ is the time. If the particle is at the position $x=0$ when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t.$\n\n$x(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c24170938204d7e3ae56ffc313b8acd227877a6a25ad915daa67a723f1c75c3"} {"question" "q-351724" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by integrating the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int {csc}^{2}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(t)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $x(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+C \\\\ 0 & =-1+C \\\\ C & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(t)+1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c24170938204d7e3ae56ffc313b8acd227877a6a25ad915daa67a723f1c75c3"} {"question" "q-351724" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7a0b1ed9ffb8f9da9229cc9c42f583e6acc8f07ec669b0a20abf12286cd89bcb"} {"question" "q-351724" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c24170938204d7e3ae56ffc313b8acd227877a6a25ad915daa67a723f1c75c3"} {"question" "q-351724" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1-\\cot(t)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c24170938204d7e3ae56ffc313b8acd227877a6a25ad915daa67a723f1c75c3"} {"question" "q-7283" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with velocity $v(t)=3{t}^{2}+2t,$ where $t$ is the time. If the particle is at the position $x=15$ when $t=2,$ calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t>0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c24170938204d7e3ae56ffc313b8acd227877a6a25ad915daa67a723f1c75c3"} {"question" "q-7283" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by integrating the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (3{t}^{2}+2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{3}+{t}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $x(2)=15.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15 & =(2)^{3}+(2)^{2}+C \\\\ 15 & =8+4+C \\\\ C & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)={t}^{3}+{t}^{2}+3.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c24170938204d7e3ae56ffc313b8acd227877a6a25ad915daa67a723f1c75c3"} {"question" "q-7283" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7a0b1ed9ffb8f9da9229cc9c42f583e6acc8f07ec669b0a20abf12286cd89bcb"} {"question" "q-7283" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c24170938204d7e3ae56ffc313b8acd227877a6a25ad915daa67a723f1c75c3"} {"question" "q-7283" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x(t)=4t+7"] ["math" "x(t)=6t+3"] ["math" "x(t)={t}^{3}+{t}^{2}"] ["math" "x(t)={t}^{3}+{t}^{2}+3"] ["math" "x(t)={t}^{3}+{t}^{2}-5"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c24170938204d7e3ae56ffc313b8acd227877a6a25ad915daa67a723f1c75c3"} {"question" "q-7283" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x(t)={t}^{3}+{t}^{2}+3"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c24170938204d7e3ae56ffc313b8acd227877a6a25ad915daa67a723f1c75c3"} {"question" "q-6255" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with velocity $v(t)=4{t}^{3}+{t}^{2}+6t,$ where $t$ is the time. If the particle is at the position $x=11$ when $t=1,$ calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t>0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c24170938204d7e3ae56ffc313b8acd227877a6a25ad915daa67a723f1c75c3"} {"question" "q-6255" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by integrating the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,dt \\\\  & =\\int (4{t}^{3}+{t}^{2}+6t)dt \\\\  & ={t}^{4}+\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}+3{t}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $x(1)=11.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11 & =(1)^{4}+\\frac{(1)^{3}}{3}+3(1)^{2}+C \\\\ 11 & =1+\\frac{1}{3}+3+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{20}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)={t}^{4}+\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}+3{t}^{2}+\\frac{20}{3}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c24170938204d7e3ae56ffc313b8acd227877a6a25ad915daa67a723f1c75c3"} {"question" "q-6255" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7a0b1ed9ffb8f9da9229cc9c42f583e6acc8f07ec669b0a20abf12286cd89bcb"} {"question" "q-6255" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c24170938204d7e3ae56ffc313b8acd227877a6a25ad915daa67a723f1c75c3"} {"question" "q-6255" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x(t)=4{t}^{3}+\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}+3{t}^{2}+\\frac{20}{3}"] ["math" "x(t)=4{t}^{3}+\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}+6t+\\frac{20}{3}"] ["math" "x(t)={t}^{4}+\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}+3{t}^{2}+\\frac{20}{3}"] ["math" "x(t)={t}^{4}+\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}+3{t}^{2}-\\frac{20}{3}"] ["math" "x(t)={t}^{4}+{t}^{2}+6t-\\frac{20}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c24170938204d7e3ae56ffc313b8acd227877a6a25ad915daa67a723f1c75c3"} {"question" "q-6255" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x(t)={t}^{4}+\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}+3{t}^{2}+\\frac{20}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c24170938204d7e3ae56ffc313b8acd227877a6a25ad915daa67a723f1c75c3"} {"question" "q-7284" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with velocity $v(t)=\\frac{1}{4}t-{t}^{2},$ where $t>0$ is the time. If the particle is at the position $x=-7$ when $t=3,$ calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c24170938204d7e3ae56ffc313b8acd227877a6a25ad915daa67a723f1c75c3"} {"question" "q-7284" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by integrating the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{1}{4}t-{t}^{2})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $x(3)=-7.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7 & =\\frac{1}{8}(3)^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}(3)^{3}+C \\\\ -7 & =-\\frac{63}{8}+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{7}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}+\\frac{7}{8}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c24170938204d7e3ae56ffc313b8acd227877a6a25ad915daa67a723f1c75c3"} {"question" "q-7284" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7a0b1ed9ffb8f9da9229cc9c42f583e6acc8f07ec669b0a20abf12286cd89bcb"} {"question" "q-7284" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c24170938204d7e3ae56ffc313b8acd227877a6a25ad915daa67a723f1c75c3"} {"question" "q-7284" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x(t)=\\frac{1}{4}-2t"] ["math" "x(t)=\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}+1"] ["math" "x(t)=\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}+4"] ["math" "x(t)=\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}+\\frac{7}{8}"] ["math" "x(t)=\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}-\\frac{63}{8}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c24170938204d7e3ae56ffc313b8acd227877a6a25ad915daa67a723f1c75c3"} {"question" "q-7284" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x(t)=\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}+\\frac{7}{8}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c24170938204d7e3ae56ffc313b8acd227877a6a25ad915daa67a723f1c75c3"} {"question" "q-7110" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with velocity $v(t)=6t+6{t}^{2},$ where $t>0$ is the time. If the particle is at the position $x=2$ when $t=0,$ calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c24170938204d7e3ae56ffc313b8acd227877a6a25ad915daa67a723f1c75c3"} {"question" "q-7110" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by integrating the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (6t+6{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}+2{t}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $x(0)=2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =3(0)^{2}+2(0)^{3}+C \\\\ C & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=3{t}^{2}+2{t}^{3}+2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c24170938204d7e3ae56ffc313b8acd227877a6a25ad915daa67a723f1c75c3"} {"question" "q-7110" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7a0b1ed9ffb8f9da9229cc9c42f583e6acc8f07ec669b0a20abf12286cd89bcb"} {"question" "q-7110" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c24170938204d7e3ae56ffc313b8acd227877a6a25ad915daa67a723f1c75c3"} {"question" "q-7110" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x(t)=3{t}^{2}+2{t}^{3}"] ["math" "x(t)=3{t}^{2}+2{t}^{3}+1"] ["math" "x(t)=3{t}^{2}+2{t}^{3}+2"] ["math" "x(t)=3{t}^{2}+2{t}^{3}-1"] ["math" "x(t)=3{t}^{2}+2{t}^{3}-2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c24170938204d7e3ae56ffc313b8acd227877a6a25ad915daa67a723f1c75c3"} {"question" "q-7110" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x(t)=3{t}^{2}+2{t}^{3}+2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c24170938204d7e3ae56ffc313b8acd227877a6a25ad915daa67a723f1c75c3"} {"question" "q-351730" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration $a(t)={e}^{3t},$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time. If the particle has position $x=0$ and velocity $v=1$ when $t=0,$ the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is\n\n$x(t)=$\n{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c24170938204d7e3ae56ffc313b8acd227877a6a25ad915daa67a723f1c75c3"} {"question" "q-351730" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by integrating the acceleration to get the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int {e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{0}+C \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{1}{3}+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the velocity is given by $v(t)=\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}+\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nNow, we integrate the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}+\\frac{2}{3})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{2}{3}\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{3t}+\\frac{2}{3}t+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $K$, we use the fact that $x(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{0}+\\frac{2}{3}(0)+K \\\\ 0 & =\\frac{1}{9}+K \\\\ K & =-\\frac{1}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{3t}+\\frac{2}{3}t-\\frac{1}{9}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c24170938204d7e3ae56ffc313b8acd227877a6a25ad915daa67a723f1c75c3"} {"question" "q-351730" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7a0b1ed9ffb8f9da9229cc9c42f583e6acc8f07ec669b0a20abf12286cd89bcb"} {"question" "q-351730" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c24170938204d7e3ae56ffc313b8acd227877a6a25ad915daa67a723f1c75c3"} {"question" "q-351730" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{e^{3t}}{9}+\\frac{2t}{3}-\\frac{1}{9}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c24170938204d7e3ae56ffc313b8acd227877a6a25ad915daa67a723f1c75c3"} {"question" "q-81441" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration $a(t)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time. If the particle has position $x=5$ and velocity $v=1$ when $t=0,$ calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c24170938204d7e3ae56ffc313b8acd227877a6a25ad915daa67a723f1c75c3"} {"question" "q-81441" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by integrating the acceleration to get the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3(0))+C \\\\ 1 & =-1+C \\\\ C & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the velocity is given by $v(t)=2-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t).$\n\nNow, we integrate the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (2-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2t-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $K,$ we use the fact that $x(0)=5.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =2(0)-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3(0))+K \\\\ K & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=2t-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+5.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c24170938204d7e3ae56ffc313b8acd227877a6a25ad915daa67a723f1c75c3"} {"question" "q-81441" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7a0b1ed9ffb8f9da9229cc9c42f583e6acc8f07ec669b0a20abf12286cd89bcb"} {"question" "q-81441" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c24170938204d7e3ae56ffc313b8acd227877a6a25ad915daa67a723f1c75c3"} {"question" "q-81441" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x(t)=2t+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+5"] ["math" "x(t)=2t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+8"] ["math" "x(t)=2t-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+5"] ["math" "x(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+4"] ["math" "x(t)=t+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+5"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c24170938204d7e3ae56ffc313b8acd227877a6a25ad915daa67a723f1c75c3"} {"question" "q-81441" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x(t)=2t-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+5"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c24170938204d7e3ae56ffc313b8acd227877a6a25ad915daa67a723f1c75c3"} {"question" "q-7111" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration $a(t)=1-6t,$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time. If the particle has position $x=2$ and velocity $v=4$ when $t=0,$ calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t>0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c24170938204d7e3ae56ffc313b8acd227877a6a25ad915daa67a723f1c75c3"} {"question" "q-7111" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by integrating the acceleration to get the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (1-6t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =t-3{t}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=4.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =(0)-3(0)^{2}+C \\\\ C & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the velocity is given by $v(t)=t-3{t}^{2}+4.$\n\nNow, we integrate the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (t-3{t}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}-{t}^{3}+4t+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $K,$ we use the fact that $x(0)=2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =\\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2}-(0)^{3}+4(0)+K \\\\ K & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}-{t}^{3}+4t+2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c24170938204d7e3ae56ffc313b8acd227877a6a25ad915daa67a723f1c75c3"} {"question" "q-7111" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7a0b1ed9ffb8f9da9229cc9c42f583e6acc8f07ec669b0a20abf12286cd89bcb"} {"question" "q-7111" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c24170938204d7e3ae56ffc313b8acd227877a6a25ad915daa67a723f1c75c3"} {"question" "q-7111" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x(t)=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}+{t}^{3}+6t-2."] ["math" "x(t)=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}-{t}^{3}+4t+2"] ["math" "x(t)=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}-{t}^{3}+6t."] ["math" "x(t)={t}^{2}+{t}^{3}+4t+2."] ["math" "x(t)={t}^{2}-{t}^{3}+2t+2."]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c24170938204d7e3ae56ffc313b8acd227877a6a25ad915daa67a723f1c75c3"} {"question" "q-7111" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x(t)=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}-{t}^{3}+4t+2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c24170938204d7e3ae56ffc313b8acd227877a6a25ad915daa67a723f1c75c3"} {"question" "q-351742" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration $a(t)=20{t}^{3}+12,$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time. If the particle has position $x=-3$ and velocity $v=1$ when $t=0,$ the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is\n\n$x(t)={t}^{5}+\\,$\n{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c24170938204d7e3ae56ffc313b8acd227877a6a25ad915daa67a723f1c75c3"} {"question" "q-351742" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by integrating the acceleration to get the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (20{t}^{3}+12)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =5{t}^{4}+12t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =0+0+C \\\\ C & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the velocity is given by $v(t)=5{t}^{4}+12t+1.$\n\nNow, we integrate the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (5{t}^{4}+12t+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{5}+6{t}^{2}+t+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $K$, we use the fact that $x(0)=-3.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3 & =0+0+0+K \\\\ K & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)={t}^{5}+6{t}^{2}+t-3.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c24170938204d7e3ae56ffc313b8acd227877a6a25ad915daa67a723f1c75c3"} {"question" "q-351742" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7a0b1ed9ffb8f9da9229cc9c42f583e6acc8f07ec669b0a20abf12286cd89bcb"} {"question" "q-351742" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c24170938204d7e3ae56ffc313b8acd227877a6a25ad915daa67a723f1c75c3"} {"question" "q-351742" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6t^2+t-3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c24170938204d7e3ae56ffc313b8acd227877a6a25ad915daa67a723f1c75c3"} {"question" "q-84244" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration $a(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t),$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time. If the particle has position $x=2\\pi $ and velocity $v=4$ when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c24170938204d7e3ae56ffc313b8acd227877a6a25ad915daa67a723f1c75c3"} {"question" "q-84244" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by integrating the acceleration to get the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=4.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+C \\\\ 4 & =2(1)+C \\\\ C & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the velocity is given by $v(t)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+2.$\n\nNow, we integrate the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+2)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+2t+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $K,$ we use the fact that $x(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=2\\pi .$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi  & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+2(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+K \\\\ 2\\pi  & =-2(0)+\\pi +K \\\\ K & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+2t+\\pi .\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c24170938204d7e3ae56ffc313b8acd227877a6a25ad915daa67a723f1c75c3"} {"question" "q-84244" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7a0b1ed9ffb8f9da9229cc9c42f583e6acc8f07ec669b0a20abf12286cd89bcb"} {"question" "q-84244" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c24170938204d7e3ae56ffc313b8acd227877a6a25ad915daa67a723f1c75c3"} {"question" "q-84244" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x(t)=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+2t+\\pi "] ["math" "x(t)=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+4t"] ["math" "x(t)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+2t+\\pi "] ["math" "x(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-2t+3\\pi "] ["math" "x(t)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+2t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c24170938204d7e3ae56ffc313b8acd227877a6a25ad915daa67a723f1c75c3"} {"question" "q-84244" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x(t)=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+2t+\\pi "] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c24170938204d7e3ae56ffc313b8acd227877a6a25ad915daa67a723f1c75c3"} {"question" "e-566" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with velocity $v(t) = 2t+3t^2,$ where $t\\geq 0$ is the time. If the particle is at the position $x=1$ when $t=0,$ calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/example-566.json" "file_sha256" "7d957ebb615aae355106efc3d71f66ff16facecf5bb29f73b038af9a289c8c8c"} {"question" "e-566" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by integrating the velocity to get $x(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (2t+3{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+{t}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $x(0) = 1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =(0)^{2}+(0)^{3}+C \\\\ C & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nx(t) = t^3+t^2+1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/example-566.json" "file_sha256" "7d957ebb615aae355106efc3d71f66ff16facecf5bb29f73b038af9a289c8c8c"} {"question" "e-567" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration $a(t) = 1-2t,$ where $t\\geq 0$ is the time. If the particle has position $x=1$ and velocity $v=2$ when $t=1,$ calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t >0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/example-567.json" "file_sha256" "fa565d9f6fbb776da62c27108739e91c883fcc178cb4da6538af09758ab0ed49"} {"question" "e-567" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by integrating the acceleration to get the velocity $v(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (1-2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =t-{t}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(1) = 2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =(1)-(1)^{2}+C \\\\ C & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the velocity is given by $v(t) = t-t^2 + 2.$\n\nNow, we integrate the velocity to get $x(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (t-{t}^{2}+2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}+2t+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $K,$ we use the fact that $x(1) = 1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =\\frac{1}{2}(1)^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}(1)^{3}+2(1)+K \\\\ K & =-\\frac{7}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t) = \\dfrac{1}{2}t^2-\\dfrac{1}{3}t^3 +2t -\\dfrac{7}{6}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/example-567.json" "file_sha256" "fa565d9f6fbb776da62c27108739e91c883fcc178cb4da6538af09758ab0ed49"}] "usage" {"e-566" [] "q-7110" [] "e-567" []} "basis" 1156 "retractions" [[17592186064852 :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nA particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with velocity $v(t) = 6t + 6t^{2}$, where $t > 0$ is the time. If the particle is at the position $x = 2$ when $t = 0$, calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$."] [17592186064023 :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with velocity $v(t) = 2t+3t^2$, where $t>0$ is the time. If the particle is at the position $x=1$ when $t=0$, calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$."] [17592186064023 :question/worked-solution "We start by integrating the velocity to get $x(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= ∫v(t)dt \\\\\n&= ∫(2t + 3t^{2})dt \\\\\n&= t^{2} + t^{3} + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $x(0) = 1$. Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 &= (0)^{2} + (0)^{3} + C \\\\\nC &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nx(t) = t^3+t^2+1\n$$"] [17592186064024 :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration $a(t) = 1-2t$, where $t\\geq 0$ is the time. If the particle has position $x=1$ and velocity $v=2$ when $t=1$, calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t >0$."] [17592186064024 :question/worked-solution "We start by integrating the acceleration to get the velocity $v(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(t) &= ∫a(t)dt \\\\\n&= ∫(1 - 2t)dt \\\\\n&= t - t^{2} + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(1) = 2$. Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2 &= (1) - (1)^{2} + C \\\\\nC &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the velocity is given by\n\n$$\nv(t) = t-t^2 + 2\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate the velocity to get $x(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= ∫v(t)dt \\\\\n&= ∫(t - t^{2} + 2)dt \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}t^{2} - \\frac{1}{3}t^{3} + 2t + K\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $K$, we use the fact that $x(1) = 1$. Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 &= \\frac{1}{2}(1)^{2} - \\frac{1}{3}(1)^{3} + 2(1) + K \\\\\nK &= -\\frac{7}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nx(t) = \\dfrac{1}{2}t^2-\\dfrac{1}{3}t^3 +2t -\\dfrac{7}{6}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186063623 17592186063624 17592186063625 17592186063626 17592186063627] "immutable_fields" [[17592186063622 :answer-field/correct] [17592186063622 :answer-field/id] [17592186063622 :answer-field/key] [17592186063622 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
